Hi Cal,

Yes, p-vals approaching 1 reported by parboot and gof functions do indicate underdispersion and hat the fitted model cannot effectively reproduce your empirical data.

I don't know of any good rules of thumb regarding specific values for underdispersion, although I guess c-hat =0.5 is as extreme as c-hat = 2. To the best of my knowledge, this hasn't really been explored very deeply for these types of models. I don't think the tests distinguish between an underdispersed state process vs. obs. process, and I'd blindly speculate that whether underdispersion matters kind of depends upon where it's happening. I dunno, some simulation (and, within your data, looking for patterns underlying the discrepancies) might be pretty useful.

Cheers,

John

