From ub07af4174ebf173b at groups.google.com  Mon Oct 21 22:41:51 2019
From: ub07af4174ebf173b at groups.google.com (Pat Taggart)
Date: Mon, 21 Oct 2019 22:41:51 +0000
Subject: Non-independence of animal observations in open populations
Message-ID: <IZlM4CZliz4.1wl5QH0sBQAJ@unmarked.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/unmarked/c/IZlM4CZliz4/m/1wl5QH0sBQAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hi All,

Thanks in advance for any and all help.

I am interested to hear if it is possible to account for non-independence of animal detections in an open populations? and if so how you might go about this?

I have 22 sites. My primary sampling periods are made up of 29 year x season combinations, 2011-2018 with 4 seasons each year except 2018 which only has 1 season. Within each primary sampling period I have 3 replicate rabbit counts. However, as rabbits live in groups in warrens the detection of one rabbit makes it more likely that additional rabbits are also detected - my data violate the assumption of 'independence of detection'.

I am keen to estimate rabbit abundance at each of my 22 sites for each year*season combination. This can be done using an open population n-mixture model using the function pcountOpen(), although I am unsure if or how I could account for the non-independence of animal detections within pcountOpen()? Alternatively, is there another way that I could estimate rabbit abundance for each year*season combination at each site whilst also accounting for non-independence of detection? Would my model be fundamentally incorrect to ignore this model assumption in this instance if it is not possible to account for non-independence of detection?

I notice that it is possible to account for non-independence of detection in closed population n-mixture models using a beta-binomial distribution to describe the observation process (Martin et al 2011; Dorazio et al 2013), but I can't seem to work out how this might or if it can be implemented in unmarked in r.

Thanks again,
Pat

From u4a635fe980c8c1cd at groups.google.com  Tue Oct 22 14:58:23 2019
