Hi Pat,

I think it is certainly possible to plug the beta-binomial observation model into an open Dail-Madsen type of model. I don't believe this is something that could be directly implemented using unmarked, but you could pull code from Dorazio et al. or Martin et al. and plug it into code for a standard open model. Maybe using BUGS (and borrowing from Martin et al.) is more intuitive than trying to hard-code the likelihood to be optimized.  Something like the below (you'd need to define priors and the process model, etc.)--hope it helps.

John

for (i in 1:R) { # Loops over R sites

   N[i, 1] ~ dpois(lambda[i])  #Initial Abundance at each site

}

for (i in 1:R) { # Loops over R sites

  for (t in 2:T) { #Loops over the subsequent time periods

   N[i, T] <-S[i, t]+R[i,t]  #survivors + recruits

   S[i, t]~dbinom(phi, N[i, t-1])

   R[i, t]~dpois(gamma*N[s, t-1])

}

}

for (i in 1:R) { # Loops over R sites

  for (t in 1:T) { #Loops over all time periods

   for (k in 1:K) { # Loops over repeated visits or whatever

# Observation model for replicated counts

C[i, t, k] ~ dbin(p[i, t, k], N[i, t])

        p[i, t, k]~dbeta(alpha.p,beta.p) ##this is the beta-binomial part

 }

 }

}

